{
  "summary": "The discussion has reached diminishing returns on extraction taxonomy and is now relitigating settled points (escaping dedup, Tier 1 naming, _check_for_changes caching) while missing two actionable observations from the actual code. First, the escaping dedup everyone agrees on is real but mechanically simpler than described: only 6 blocks in _build_feature_detail_widgets (lines 336, 345, 354, 371, 380, 389) use the inline pattern while 8 other callsites in the same function already use _escape_markup correctly (lines 326, 331, 396, 403, 431, 436, 449, 475), proving the function was refactored partway and left inconsistent — this is a 10-minute cleanup, not a design decision, and framing it as 'the highest-value first commit' is overthinking a mechanical fix. Second, _load_features (line 2506) is a one-liner delegating to FeatureFile.list_all(board=board), and the double-call at lines 2606/2636 calls it with different arguments (board-filtered vs. unfiltered) — so the 'pass already-loaded features to server push' suggestion from the seventh contributor would require loading all-boards features from the board-filtered set plus the remaining boards, which is not actually simpler than just calling list_all() twice. The honest fix is that FeatureFile.list_all() does a directory glob + JSON parse per call with no caching whatsoever (state.py line 557), so any caching strategy must live in FeatureFile itself — a simple TTL-based instance cache (cache parsed FeatureFile by path+mtime, expire after 10s matching the refresh interval) would halve the parse cost with zero API changes. The broader meta-observation is that this ideation has stalled twice and is now in its fourth cycle because participants keep proposing new sequencing frameworks instead of acknowledging that Tier 1 is a straightforward mechanical extraction that any competent developer can execute in an afternoon without further design discussion — the value of continued ideation on this item is near zero."
}
